Q: how to use and update panels in ncursesw A: <p>For me, the full recipe for the <strong>wide</strong> version looks like this:</p>
<p>You need to full switch to ncursesw/</p>
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>#include &lt;ncursesw/panel.h&gt;
#include &lt;ncursesw/ncurses.h&gt;
#include &lt;ncursesw/menu.h&gt;
#include &lt;ncursesw/form.h&gt;
#include &lt;locale.h&gt;
// compatible for all langs
setlocale(LC_ALL, &quot;&quot;); // main ...
// Also use wchar_t and %ls
</code></pre>
<p>And use full 'W' alternatives for libs like:</p>
<pre class="lang-bash prettyprint-override"><code>gcc main.c -lncursesw -lpanelw -lmenuw -lformw -DNCURSES_WIDECHAR
</code></pre>

Q: package specified with braces gives error when it contains a __DATA__ section A: <p>You can have <code>__DATA__</code> on a per-package basis like this:</p>
<p>in <code>foo.pm</code>:</p>
<pre class="lang-perl prettyprint-override"><code>use strict;
use warnings;

package foo;
1;

__DATA__
foo data
</code></pre>
<p>and same in <code>bar.pm</code>:</p>
<pre class="lang-perl prettyprint-override"><code>use strict;
use warnings;

package bar;
1;

__DATA__
bar data
</code></pre>
<p>plus a program to call these:</p>
<pre class="lang-perl prettyprint-override"><code>use strict;
use warnings;
use foo;
use bar;

while (&lt;foo::DATA&gt;) {
    print;
}
while (&lt;bar::DATA&gt;) {
    print;
}
</code></pre>
<p>Run with <code>-I.</code> to point it to the packages:</p>
<pre class="lang-bash prettyprint-override"><code>$ perl -I. mcve.pl
foo data
bar data
</code></pre>
<p>I only use <code>__DATA__</code> for scripts where I want to include some data with the script and avoid external files. I'd probably use classes and functions or constants to share data from my modules, but you <em>could</em> do it like this.</p>
<p>If you want multiple packages in the same file, you can't use <code>__DATA__</code> multiple times, as toolic explained. Maybe a <a href="https://perldoc.perl.org/perlop#Here-docs" rel="nofollow noreferrer">here document</a> would be a better fit for such cases, or by hiding the data behind a function.</p>
<pre class="lang-perl prettyprint-override"><code>use strict;
use warnings;

package foo;
our $foo_data = &lt;&lt;HERE;
foo data
HERE

package main;
print $foo::foo_data;
</code></pre>
